The most contentious real-world question when applying Clean to Spring. Three options:

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Share 1 class</th><th>Split into 2 classes + mapper</th><th>Shared but hides JPA</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Boilerplate</td><td>none</td><td><b>a lot</b> (two-way mapper)</td><td>little</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clean domain</td><td>no — tied to JPA</td><td><b>completely clean</b></td><td>mostly clean</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invariants</td><td>hard — JPA needs a no-arg ctor + setters</td><td>protected</td><td>partially protected</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lazy loading</td><td>leaks outside the domain</td><td>doesn't leak</td><td>still leaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Good fit when</td><td>CRUD, thin domain</td><td>complex, long-lived domain</td><td>middle ground</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

**Why sharing hurts:** JPA requires a no-arg constructor and setters so it can reconstruct the object → the domain can no longer enforce its invariants. On top of that, a lazy `@OneToMany` turns a plain property read in the domain into an actual SQL query, and `LazyInitializationException` blows up in a layer that has nothing to do with the DB.

<pre><code><span class="c">// domain/Booking.java — no annotations at all</span>
<span class="k">public final class</span> Booking {
    <span class="k">private final</span> BookingId id;
    <span class="k">private</span> BookingStatus status;
    <span class="k">private final</span> List&lt;Passenger&gt; passengers;
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public void</span> <span class="f">cancel</span>() {                       <span class="c">// invariant lives in the domain</span>
        <span class="k">if</span> (status == ISSUED) <span class="k">throw new</span> DomainException(<span class="s">"Ticket already issued, must be refunded"</span>);
        status = CANCELLED;
    }
}
<span class="c">// adapter/out/persistence/BookingJpaEntity.java — pure storage</span>
<span class="k">@Entity @Table</span>(name = <span class="s">"bookings"</span>)
<span class="k">class</span> BookingJpaEntity {
    <span class="k">@Id</span> <span class="k">private</span> String id;
    <span class="k">@Enumerated</span>(STRING) <span class="k">private</span> BookingStatus status;
    <span class="k">protected</span> <span class="f">BookingJpaEntity</span>() {}          <span class="c">// JPA needs it — but never exposed to the domain</span>
}</code></pre>

:::deep
- Hand-written mappers are error-prone and tedious → use [[g:MapStruct]] (generates code at compile time, no reflection at runtime). This is a case where a library is worth it.
- The real cost of splitting is [[o:not]] the mapper — it's when the domain and the table diverge significantly (an aggregate spanning 4 tables). That's when the repository adapter has to assemble/split them — and that's exactly what it exists to do.
- [[r:Half-hearted trap]]: splitting into 2 classes but letting the domain's `getId()` return the DB's auto-increment `Long`. The domain should use its own id (UUID/business code), generated in the domain — see 17.1.
- If you choose to share: at minimum keep setters package-private, add `@Access(FIELD)`, and [[g:forbid `EAGER`]] — reduces leakage without paying for a mapper.
- Decide [[o:per bounded context]], not for the whole repo: Booking/Payment get split; a simple airport-code lookup table can share a class, or even skip a domain model entirely.
- Common trick question: *"does the repository return the domain object or the entity?"* — [[g:always the domain object]]. If it returns the entity, that port isn't really a port.
:::

[[g:One class per use case]] — this is the biggest difference from an 800-line `BookingService` in the layered style.

<pre><code><span class="c">// port/in — the contract, reading it tells you what the system can do</span>
<span class="k">public interface</span> HoldSeatUseCase {
    HoldResult <span class="f">hold</span>(HoldSeatCommand cmd);
}
<span class="c">// application/service — orchestration, contains NO business rules</span>
<span class="k">@Service</span>
<span class="k">class</span> HoldSeatService <span class="k">implements</span> HoldSeatUseCase {
    <span class="k">private final</span> BookingRepository bookings;   <span class="c">// port/out</span>
    <span class="k">private final</span> SeatLockPort locks;
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">@Override</span> <span class="k">@Transactional</span>                    <span class="c">// the transaction boundary is HERE</span>
    <span class="k">public</span> HoldResult <span class="f">hold</span>(HoldSeatCommand cmd) {
        <span class="k">if</span> (!locks.<span class="f">tryLock</span>(cmd.<span class="f">flightId</span>(), cmd.<span class="f">seatNo</span>())) <span class="k">return</span> HoldResult.<span class="f">taken</span>();
        Booking b = bookings.<span class="f">findById</span>(cmd.<span class="f">bookingId</span>())
                            .<span class="f">orElseThrow</span>(NotFound::<span class="k">new</span>);
        b.<span class="f">holdSeat</span>(cmd.<span class="f">seatNo</span>());                <span class="c">// the rule lives in the DOMAIN</span>
        bookings.<span class="f">save</span>(b);
        <span class="k">return</span> HoldResult.<span class="f">ok</span>(b.<span class="f">holdExpiresAt</span>());
    }
}</code></pre>

**Clear boundary:** the use case *orchestrates* (fetch → call the domain → save → publish event). The business decision of *"can this seat be held"* lives inside `Booking`, not here. If a use case is full of business `if` statements, the domain is anemic (12.11 anemic domain).

**Command/Result are the application layer's DTOs**, separate from the web layer's DTOs (`adapter/in/web`). This sounds redundant, but it lets you change the REST contract (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4) without touching the use case, and lets the same use case run from a Kafka consumer.

:::deep
- A Command should [[g:self-validate its syntax]] right in the constructor (a `record` compact constructor) — it should be impossible to create a meaningless `HoldSeatCommand`. This is the "make invalid state unrepresentable" style.
- A use case calling another use case is a [[r:smell]]: either extract the shared part down into a domain service, or use a domain event. Nested calls muddy the transaction and make errors hard to trace.
- [[o:Don't]] let a use case return a raw JPA entity or domain object to the controller — return a `Result` so the controller doesn't depend on the domain's shape.
- A read-only (query) use case usually [[g:doesn't need to go through the domain]]: read directly via a projection for speed. That's the natural gateway into CQRS (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4) without doing full event sourcing.
- Class names should be [[g:business verbs]]: `HoldSeat`, `IssueTicket`, `RefundBooking`. Reading the file list in `port/in` tells you what the system does — 'screaming architecture' (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8).
- Quick quality check: `application/service` imports should only include `domain`, `port`, and transaction annotations. Seeing `javax.persistence` or `HttpServletRequest` here means it's already broken.
:::
